course: dictionary
lesson: 10355
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Transportation is a noun, and it can have more than one meaning. In everyday life it means moving people or goods from one place to another, or a way of travelling or carrying things. Cars, buses, trains, planes and ships are all transportation.
explain: Read this context: «From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent about 160,000 convicts on ships to Australia, a punishment called transportation, sometimes for quite small thefts.» The clues are convicts, punishment and thefts. Here transportation does not mean travelling. It means sending people far away to live and work as a punishment.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Try "a punishment called travelling by bus": it does not make sense. Try "a punishment called sending people far away": it fits. The clues were right.
explain: In the USA, people use transportation every day. In the UK and Australia, people more often say transport. The two words mean much the same in everyday use, so you can use either one.
fact: Transportation means moving people or goods from one place to another.
fact: Transportation can also mean a way of travelling or carrying things, such as cars, buses, trains, planes and ships.
fact: In history, transportation was a punishment: convicts were sent far away to live and work.
fact: From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent convicts to Australia, and earlier to America.
fact: In science and geography, transportation is the movement of rock and soil by rivers, wind and ice.
exercise: In everyday life, what is transportation? | A punishment for a crime | *Cars, buses, trains, planes and ships that carry people or goods | A rock moved by a river | A type of soil found in the hills
exercise: «From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent convicts on ships to Australia, a punishment called transportation.» What does transportation mean here? | Building new roads in a city | Carrying food to a shop | Holidays by boat | *Sending people far away to live and work as a punishment
exercise: Which word in «a punishment called transportation» is the clue that it means a punishment? | ships | Australia | Britain | *punishment
test: Which of these is an example of transportation in the everyday sense? | *Ferry | Hat | Pencil | Lamp
test: Some people in the UK say «transport» instead of «transportation». What does this show? | Transport only means a punishment | *The two words are used differently in different places but mean much the same | The two words mean opposite things | Transport is a type of rock
exam: In geography, what is transportation? | Sending convicts to Australia | Rain falling from the sky | A way of carrying people in cars | *The movement of rock and soil by rivers, wind and ice
